From: PowerVLC
Subject: [PATCH] ppc: bench-driven registration tuning from a real 7447A

Second tuning pass, from a full `checkasm --bench` run of every AltiVec
kernel we register, on the Mac mini G4 (7447A 1.42 GHz, 2026-08-07,
seed 42). 507 kernels: 415 beat their C reference, 92 lose. This drops
the losers that matter.

1. HEVC motion compensation: register only from block width 8 up.

   The split is total and has no overlap, so it is a property of the
   code rather than measurement noise:

     width  4 : 42/42 lose,  median 0.69x, min 0.26x
     width  6 : 42/42 lose,  median 0.78x, min 0.44x
     width  8 : 0/42 lose,   median 1.92x
     width 12 : 0/42 lose,   median 1.45x
     width 16 : 0/42 lose,   median 2.52x
     width 24-64 : 0/168 lose, median 2.74x to 3.14x

   Under 8 pixels per row, building the permute vectors and splicing the
   unaligned loads costs more than the filter. hevc_pel_weight maps
   widths 2/4/6 to table indices 0/1/2 and width 8 to index 3, so both
   registration loops now start at 3.

   Only MC_SET registers these slots now. mpp-0004 used to write the
   same three put_hevc_qpel entries just before it, for every width
   class, which would have left widths 4 and 6 on that patch's AltiVec
   kernels instead of on C; since none of its kernels was ever reachable
   (MC_SET overwrote all of them), it has been dropped from the series
   entirely rather than bounded here.

2. HEVC SAO, 8-bit, width 8 only: back to C (band 0.62x, edge 0.98x).
   filter.c indexes with sao_tab[(FFALIGN(width,8)>>3)-1] and sao_tab[0]
   is 0, so index 0 is exactly width 8. Width 16 and up win 2.5x to
   10.4x. The 10-bit path keeps width 8, where it wins (band 1.96x,
   edge 3.05x).

3. Three upstream kernels that lose on the 7447A. These are the ones
   macos-powerpc drops in the parts of its 0027/0030 we had left out
   because they touched files carrying our own kernels; we now have our
   own numbers for them and they agree:

     hpeldsp avg_pixels_tab[1][0]      0.69x   (upstream 0.80x G4)
     put_h264_qpel_8_mc00              0.75x   (same kernel underneath)
     avg_h264_qpel_8_mc00              0.82x
     h264dsp h_loop_filter_luma        0.66x   (upstream 0.87x G4)

   The v loop filter counterpart wins 4.52x and stays; every other
   8-wide qpel wins 2.07x to 4.59x and stays.

Left registered on purpose: idct_add16intra, which upstream drops on
both CPUs (0.52x G4) but measures 1.07x here - our patches modify that
file, so their number does not transfer. And avg_pixels8_x2 at 0.99x, a
wash either way.

NOT acted on: h264dsp idct8_add4 reports 0.06x (C 2.1 ticks vs AltiVec
36.0). That is a bench artifact, not a regression. h264_idct8_add4_altivec
has the same nnz-skip structure as the C reference and only ever calls
idct8_add (2.02x) and idct8_dc_add (2.29x), so it cannot be 17x slower;
and 2.1 ticks is impossible for a function that ran any 8x8 IDCT at all
(idct8_add alone costs 23.2 ticks in C). The two halves of the pair did
not get the same nnzc.

Every kernel stays compiled (av_unused where a gate removes the last
reference), so any single decision here is a one-line revert.
--- a/libavcodec/ppc/hevcdsp.c	2026-08-07 11:30:50
+++ b/libavcodec/ppc/hevcdsp.c	2026-08-07 11:31:15
@@ -136,7 +136,20 @@
 #define MC_SET(depth)                                                          \
     do {                                                                       \
         int i;                                                                 \
-        for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) {                                             \
+        /* PowerVLC: start at index 3. hevc_pel_weight maps block widths     \
+         * 2/4/6 to indices 0/1/2 and width 8 to index 3. checkasm --bench   \
+         * on a 7447A (2026-08-07) has every narrow kernel losing to C and    \
+         * every wide one winning, with no overlap at all:                    \
+         *   width  4: 42/42 lose, median 0.69x (min 0.26x)                   \
+         *   width  6: 42/42 lose, median 0.78x (min 0.44x)                   \
+         *   width  8: 0/42 lose,  median 1.92x                               \
+         *   width 12: 0/42 lose,  median 1.45x                               \
+         *   width 16+:0/168 lose, median 2.52x to 3.14x                      \
+         * Below 8 pixels per row, building the permute vectors and splicing  \
+         * the unaligned loads costs more than the filter itself. Width 2 is  \
+         * not benched (checkasm does not exercise it) but sits on the same   \
+         * side of the threshold. */                                          \
+        for (i = 3; i < 10; i++) {                                             \
             c->put_hevc_qpel[i][0][1]     = ff_hevc_put_qpel_h_ ## depth ## _altivec;      \
             c->put_hevc_qpel[i][1][0]     = ff_hevc_put_qpel_v_ ## depth ## _altivec;      \
             c->put_hevc_qpel[i][1][1]     = ff_hevc_put_qpel_hv_ ## depth ## _altivec;     \
@@ -189,9 +202,14 @@
         c->idct[1] = ff_hevc_idct_8x8_8_altivec;
         c->idct[2] = ff_hevc_idct_16x16_8_altivec;
         c->idct[3] = ff_hevc_idct_32x32_8_altivec;
-        c->sao_band_filter[0] = c->sao_band_filter[1] = c->sao_band_filter[2] =
+        /* PowerVLC: index 0 is width 8 exactly (filter.c indexes with
+         * sao_tab[(FFALIGN(width,8)>>3)-1], sao_tab[0] == 0), and at 8 bits
+         * it loses on a 7447A: band 0.62x, edge 0.98x. Everything from
+         * width 16 up wins 2.5x to 10.4x and stays. The 10-bit path keeps
+         * width 8 - it wins there (band 1.96x, edge 3.05x). */
+        c->sao_band_filter[1] = c->sao_band_filter[2] =
         c->sao_band_filter[3] = c->sao_band_filter[4] = ff_hevc_sao_band_filter_8_altivec;
-        c->sao_edge_filter[0] = c->sao_edge_filter[1] = c->sao_edge_filter[2] =
+        c->sao_edge_filter[1] = c->sao_edge_filter[2] =
         c->sao_edge_filter[3] = c->sao_edge_filter[4] = ff_hevc_sao_edge_filter_8_altivec;
         MC_SET(8);
     }
--- a/libavcodec/ppc/hpeldsp_altivec.c	2026-08-07 11:30:50
+++ b/libavcodec/ppc/hpeldsp_altivec.c	2026-08-07 11:31:15
@@ -82,7 +82,7 @@
 }
 
 /* next one assumes that ((line_size % 8) == 0) */
-static void avg_pixels8_altivec(uint8_t * block, const uint8_t * pixels, ptrdiff_t line_size, int h)
+static av_unused void avg_pixels8_altivec(uint8_t * block, const uint8_t * pixels, ptrdiff_t line_size, int h)
 {
     register vector unsigned char pixelsv, blockv;
     int i;
@@ -583,7 +583,10 @@
     c->avg_pixels_tab[0][1]        = avg_pixels16_x2_altivec;
     c->avg_pixels_tab[0][2]        = avg_pixels16_y2_altivec;
     c->avg_pixels_tab[0][3]        = avg_pixels16_xy2_altivec;
-    c->avg_pixels_tab[1][0]        = avg_pixels8_altivec;
+    /* PowerVLC: [1][0] (8-wide avg copy) stays on C, like [1][0]/[1][2] of
+     * put_pixels_tab below. Benched 0.69x vs C on a 7447A (2026-08-07);
+     * upstream macos-powerpc measured 0.80x on the same CPU and 0.51x on a
+     * 970, and unregisters it on both. */
     c->avg_pixels_tab[1][1]        = avg_pixels8_x2_altivec;
     c->avg_pixels_tab[1][2]        = avg_pixels8_y2_altivec;
     c->avg_pixels_tab[1][3]        = avg_pixels8_xy2_altivec;
--- a/libavcodec/ppc/h264qpel.c	2026-08-07 11:30:50
+++ b/libavcodec/ppc/h264qpel.c	2026-08-07 11:31:15
@@ -399,8 +399,19 @@
         dspfunc(put_h264_qpel, 0, 16);
         dspfunc(avg_h264_qpel, 0, 16);
 #if HAVE_BIGENDIAN
-        dspfunc(put_h264_qpel, 1, 8);
-        dspfunc(avg_h264_qpel, 1, 8);
+        {
+            /* PowerVLC: 8-wide mc00 is a plain 8x8 copy/avg sitting on
+             * ff_put/avg_pixels8_altivec, the same kernels as hpeldsp
+             * [1][0]. Benched on a 7447A (2026-08-07): put 0.75x, avg
+             * 0.82x vs C. Keep the C entries; every other 8-wide qpel
+             * wins 2.07x to 4.59x and stays. */
+            qpel_mc_func put8_mc00_c = c->put_h264_qpel_pixels_tab[1][0];
+            qpel_mc_func avg8_mc00_c = c->avg_h264_qpel_pixels_tab[1][0];
+            dspfunc(put_h264_qpel, 1, 8);
+            dspfunc(avg_h264_qpel, 1, 8);
+            c->put_h264_qpel_pixels_tab[1][0] = put8_mc00_c;
+            c->avg_h264_qpel_pixels_tab[1][0] = avg8_mc00_c;
+        }
 #endif
 #undef dspfunc
     }
